
StormTracker is a React Native mobile application that helps users:

1. Search and view 7-day weather forecasts (including wind speed and temperature).

2. Document storm events with photos, storm type, notes, location, and date.

3.Save storm reports locally on their device for later reference.


Features:

Search for a city or location.

Navigate to the weather forecast or storm documentation.

1.Home Screen:
Displays daily temperature, weather conditions, and wind speed.

Search for a city or location using Search button

Shows a 7-day weather forecast for the selected location.

2.Storm Documentation Screen
Capture and upload storm images using the camera.

Enter notes and select storm type from a dropdown.

Automatically fetch and display the current location.

Choose the storm date using a date picker.

Save the entry locally on the device using AsyncStorage.

View a list of saved storm entries.

3.SavedEntries screen:

Can see all the entries saved locally 


Tech Stack
React Native with Expo

React Navigation for multi-screen routing

AsyncStorage for local storage

@react-native-picker/picker for dropdowns

@react-native-community/datetimepicker for date selection

expo-image-picker and expo-location for camera and GPS access

OpenWeatherMap API for live weather data


UI Highlights
Modern, clean interface with a background image on the Storm screen and simple background on main Home screen 
Styled inputs, buttons, and storm entry cards.


Installation:

git clone https://github.com/your-username/weather-app-expo.git
cd weather-app-react-native
npm install
npx expo start
Make sure you have the Expo CLI installed globally:
npm install -g expo-cli



Project Structure
/assets
  └── images/         # Images required
/screens
  ├── HomeScreen.js    // Main screen
  ├── SavedEntries.js     // to check saved strom Entries
  └── StormDocumentationScreen.js // to captures stroms and save data locally
/Navigation
  └── appNavigation.js
/utils
  └── asyncStorage.js
App.js

Permissions Required:
Location Access
Camera: To capture storm images
Gallery: to choose from previous images

